 well that's that's a good observation  the cynics were very  cynical about society and uh thoreau and  emerson  uh certainly had that uh aspect to their  writings  i think that um uh i think that  more research would be uh would be  interesting in this topic because  if we look back to why the cynics were  um anti-society you know they  i remember they they walked in the  streets they were homeless they  didn't go by social conventions  thoreau and emerson were somewhat social  but i think that they also valued the  wildness  or the wilderness uh very much like some  of their contemporaries at the time you  know whitman also wrote about uh the  value of nature  and um i think so i think that so to  answer your question i think they were  their own flavor of cynics  they uh they had big questions about  society  all right so there's um two questions  here actually about  throws civil disobedience  let's take the the more broad one first  then we'll come back to doug's  in in a minute for this from mark do you  see any stoic influences  in thoreau's civil disobedience  stoic influence in civil disobedience  well um  in my mind i'm connecting the idea of  what basically what  thoreau was telling us is that if  um if the laws that society give us  are immoral that we should we should  protest them  and we should work against them and of  course thoreau is also famous for ending  up in jail for not paying his  taxes uh but um that was his way of  protesting in a way  [Music]  i also think that i'm also going back in  my mind that i think there's a quote by  marcus aurelius  that says that if i have to choose  between  the roman law and the higher laws  that i will choose the latter i've i'm  i'm probably paraphrasing  marcus aurelius there but it's the same  concept is that if there is a law that's  immoral  that we need to um we need to protest  so that's actually a good lead-in to  doug's  about throws civil disobedience how  would you compare  that to stoic views on the duty of the  individual  in society i think some people just to  piggyback off of that  might view stoicism as saying well you  should be a conformist  don't rock the boat you know uh stick to  your little  place and and don't cause any any issues  but that's not really the stoic view  right  correct uh in fact i i remember uh one  of my favorite quotes about this is by  massimo  where he talks about that you know in a  recent talk in the pandemic massimo was  saying that uh  stoics are not passive we have to  remember that we are active  in every aspect of our lives and though  we may seem to be uh sitting there  passively  but actually what we're doing is we're  thinking about what am i going to say  next what am i going to do next  so we are employing reason in that  regard  and of course when thoreau wrote civil  disobedience there were a lot of things  going on  at the time in american culture  he was very much against slavery  and uh he didn't just talk about it or  lecture about it  he did participate in the underground  slave  i forget what that was called the the  movement to get slaves out of the south  the railroad yeah or the railroad right  you know so i'm going to read dan's in  just a moment but that reminds me of  something when i i needed to get  civil disobedience because i was  teaching it in class so i got  a library copy and in there there's a  really interesting  essay where he is praising john brown  after john brown's raid on harper's  ferry  and a lot of people in the north were  even condemning john brown  basically saying well we agree with his  sentiments you know we're against  slavery  but you know you can't engage in armed  insurrection  and thoreau praises him um  so i'm going to ask you this do you  think he's being is he  self-contradictory and praising  john brown for what he's engaging in or  is this a matter of  uh following principle yeah this is a  a difficult subject for american  transcendentalists but i must admit  that thoreau was praising someone that i  think  thoreau had mixed feelings about  i think later in his journals he has  some comments that he he didn't like  what john brown was doing of course  because there was  a lot of violence and death there um  i think that basically what happened was  is both thoreau and emerson  were praising john brown in one aspect  for not accepting the way things are  but they also had reservations about the  violence that he used  so uh they had mixed feelings but the  of course the essays and the writings  that have come down through history  uh we can cherry pick just one side and  say well he  he supported him but i think there was a  mixed feeling there  also for those of you who are um i  forget i think it's on  hbo or showtime there's actually a  series on john brown  that is playing weekly on sundays right  now  and i think ethan hawke is the um is the  actor who plays the lead role  so uh that's uh one way you can get  involved with  modern philosophy is looking back on  that history  well that's that's nice because it's so  rare that you get philosophy  in popular culture in that way  so here's a question from dan hayes what  have you gained personally from your  study of the transcendentalists and how  have you  implemented that in your day-to-day life  are there any practices that come  directly  from them yeah it's uh i enjoy nature  very much also  and so i i do in this pandemic i've been  walking a lot in parks  um and uh and sometimes i just sit  in a park you know where they have a  convenient bench and i'll just look at  the sky  and the trees a real appreciation for  nature and it's  um it's something that i've i've always  done through my life but now i make it a  regular practice  and i find that in this pandemic when  often we're  stuck at home a lot or more than we  would like  uh going out to a park even a park that  is  empty sometimes there are parks you may  find in your area that  have very few people at but still you  can enjoy the park for its nature  that's interesting is is it possible to  combine that with our as the stoics  teach us our social  communal nature um you know  by socially distancing walks  in parks or things like that yes  the orlando stoics started years ago  with  uh one walk per month it was on i think  the  second or third sunday but over time it  got more popular  and then we had it on every sunday um  which and best of all the members  actually suggested different parks in  orlando that were  extremely beautiful um you know like  we have a park near princeton street  it's called um  lock haven park and it's got such old  trees and interesting landscaping and an  irregular shaped  lake that we can walk around there's  also part of  part of the trip is a boardwalk part of  it is sidewalks  it's such a beautiful area and of course  we're in the rainy season now  where it rains every afternoon so we had  to put a little  hiatus on the on the walks but  uh the orlando stoics did practice  walking meditations every sunday  and we're going to get back to it also  this might actually be a good  time for dan hayes to just say a little  bit about  experimenting with exactly that that  same thing up here in milwaukee  obviously  you can't do it year round like you can  in orlando  but uh so  i came to it from my time when i was  living  in the san francisco bay area and i was  hanging out with  the fremont stoics and we went to lake  elizabeth  and we walked around there uh weekly and  so  i tried to take that and bring it back  with me when i move back here to  milwaukee  in order to try to you know recreate  this  experience of interacting with nature  and then interacting with  um some of the stoic teachings as well  as building community  great and also i'll add one thing i  found  that i didn't expect to find but in the  walking meditations that we have in  orlando  uh the people who come there tend to be  not the ones who come to the  formal discussions and it's because they  just want to talk about  they don't want to have an agenda or  anything planned out like i usually do  in my outlines but they want to just  have a free  discussion maybe about stoicism or  philosophy in general  maybe other topics maybe they just want  to ask me hey i'm getting  interested in stoicism what can you tell  me about it to get me started  so it's very important i think for our  group that  the walking meditations are a  non-structured format that gives people  the freedom to cover a lot of what they  want to talk about  that's actually a good point that i  think maybe we'll take up a little bit  more  in the panel towards the end of the day  i'm talking about like what sort of  formats and  whether it's better to have one or  several different kinds of  events throughout the month for for  stoic  meetups  great any other last questions  for for dan while we've got him here to  tap his  mind about stoicism and the  transcendentalists  we'll give it just a minute and uh if we  don't have any then then we'll  we'll sign off a little bit early we're  going to have a brief break  and then we'll be bringing up our second  speaker  meredith cons who's be talking about  a quick start guide to stoic parenting  i'll do an introduction of her  at that time it doesn't look likely like  we have any other questions so  if i had a applause and clapping  uh sound thing handy i'd be playing that  right now  but people can weigh in and chat if they  want to  thanks very much for for coming and  giving this  this talk yeah thanks for the  opportunity  you
 

